Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum number of properly-connected ordered triples over all 5-colorings of the edges of $K_{36}$.

## Setup

We have a complete graph $K_{36}$ whose edges are 5-colored (each color = one airline). An ordered triple $(A,B,C)$ of distinct cities is **properly-connected** if $\text{color}(AB) = \text{color}(BC)$.

**Total ordered triples of distinct cities:** $36 \cdot 35 \cdot 34$.

## Counting via the middle vertex

Fix the middle city $B$. Let $d_1(B), d_2(B), \ldots, d_5(B)$ be the degrees of each color at $B$, so $\sum_{i=1}^5 d_i(B) = 35$. The number of ordered pairs $(A,C)$ with $A \neq C$, both neighbors of $B$, having the same color on $BA$ and $BC$ is:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{5} d_i(B)\bigl(d_i(B)-1\bigr).$$

Summing over all 36 choices of $B$, the total number of properly-connected triples is:

$$T = \sum_{B} \sum_{i=1}^{5} d_i(B)\bigl(d_i(B)-1\bigr).$$

## Lower bound

By convexity (Cauchy–Schwarz), for each $B$:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{5} d_i(B)^2 \geq \frac{\left(\sum_i d_i(B)\right)^2}{5} = \frac{35^2}{5} = 245,$$

with equality iff all $d_i(B) = 7$ (since $35/5 = 7$). Therefore:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{5} d_i(B)\bigl(d_i(B)-1\bigr) = \sum_i d_i(B)^2 - 35 \geq 245 - 35 = 210.$$

Summing over all 36 vertices:

$$T \geq 36 \times 210 = 7560.$$

## Achieving the bound

We show a 5-coloring exists where every vertex has degree exactly 7 in each color, achieving $T = 7560$.

Since $36$ is even, $K_{36}$ admits a **1-factorization**: its $630$ edges decompose into $35$ perfect matchings (1-factors). Group these $35$ matchings into $5$ groups of $7$ matchings each. Assign one airline (color) to each group. Each group of $7$ perfect matchings forms a **7-regular spanning subgraph**, so every vertex has degree $7$ in every color.

This gives $d_i(B) = 7$ for all $i$ and all $B$, achieving equality: $T = 36 \times 5 \times 7 \times 6 = 7560$.

## Conclusion

The largest $k$ such that every arrangement yields at least $k$ properly-connected triples is:

$$\boxed{k = 7560}.$$

